  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project Outcome</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increasing capacity of energy-efficient green data centers in Thailand supported.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 1</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual PUEc of the data center is at most 1.50 (2023 baseline: not applicable) 
Data Source: PUE is a ratio metric used to determine the energy efficiency of a data center, calculated by dividing the total amount of power entering a data center by the power used to run the IT equipment within it. PUE was introduced in 2006 and promoted by The Green Grid (a non-profit organization of IT professionals) in 2007, and has become the most commonly used metric for reporting the energy efficiency of data centers. A lower PUE indicates a more energy-efficient data center. Best practice PUE ratios in Southeast Asia range from 1.4 to 1.7, higher than the most cutting-edge data centers globally because of the significant climate cooling requirements in the tropics. For the project, during operations the PUE could be higher than the designed PUE. For example, when the occupancy of the data center is low, it is likely that the PUE during that operating period will be higher than 1.4. However, the target PUE (at most 1.5) is still lower than the global standard of 1.6. 
Actual value:</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="0" year="2023"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2030-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2030-12-31"></period-end>
     <target value="0"></target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 2</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual sold capacity is at least 20 MW (2023 baseline: not applicable) 
Data Source: Data centers sell capacity in MW because power consumption is a key metric in determining the size, capacity, and operational costs of the facility 
Actual value:</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="0" year="2023"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2030-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2030-12-31"></period-end>
     <target value="0"></target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 3</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Renewable energy is procured for 50% of the annual customer electricity consumption of the data center (2023 baseline: not applicable). 
Data Source: The share of renewable energy in the annual electricity consumption of the data center will be verified using renewable energy certificates or an equivalent renewable attribution standard. 
Actual value:</narrative>
    </description>
